Z||000000||alexes||07-04-2001||09:41 AM||||I have a 1984 fiero,automatic trans 2.5<BR>the car ran great for a long time suddenly<BR>it would not start when it was raining and now it has a strong fuel odor comming from it when it is running or just sitting and not running I have no idea what the problem is can any one give me any help. I love this car and hate to have it just sitting there. Thanks||||reg||9
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||07-04-2001||10:59 AM||||Better check ALL your fuel lines ASAP!<BR>Sounds like you have a bad hose.<BR>NOT GOOD!<P>Also the fuel evaporation system.<p>[This message has been edited by Indiana_resto_guy (edited 07-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroJoe||07-04-2001||11:58 PM||||Yes, if you have a leak you better find it right now, and stop trying to crank the engine over until you find out what's leaking.  All of those fumes hanging around inside a engine compartment are just waiting to be ignited by a stray spark from the ignition system, or any other electrical device in there!||||reg||4
Z||000003||84Bill||07-05-2001||09:06 AM||||Like resto guy said check you fuel lines and in particular your vapor caniser. Every so often the purge valve on the top will stick closed and the engine vaccume will not be able to purge the canister.||||reg||14
Z||000004||Bob Englert||07-05-2001||04:13 PM||||I recently smelled gas after driving my daughter's 87 coupe.  After looking around I noticed that one of the fuel lines at the fitting just under the battery was wet with fuel.  After letting it sit and evaporate I tuned the key and it was leaking around the fitting.  It turned out the fitting had come loose and I could unscrew the connector by hand.  After tightening it up the leak was fixed.<P>I am very glad that I was driving the car and not my daughter (away at school).  I would hate to think what could have happened.  <P>Let the car sit overnight so it is cool and the gas has evaporated.  Then turn the ignition on (don't try to start it) for a few seconds at a time for the fuel pump to cycle and pressurize the fuel lines.  Then check all lines and the vapor canister carefully for any fuel leaks.  Pay particular attention to the connections between the hard and rubber fuel lines and the entire length of the rubber lines.  Hopefully the leak will be easy to spot line my was.<P>Good luck...<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
